<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="Pro5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Procedure</title>

  
<table>
    <tabular halign="center" top="medium">
        <row left="medium" bottom="medium">
        <cell right="medium" colspan="2">Materials:</cell>
        </row>
        <row left="medium" bottom="medium">
        <cell right="medium">1 Pound of Beans</cell>
        <cell right="medium">Scale</cell>
        </row>
        <row left="medium" bottom="medium">
        <cell right="medium">Weighing Tray</cell>
        <cell right="medium">Calculator</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Understanding the Biological Context</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            Each seed in your bag is a living organism, collected from a mature population of annual plants. 
            If sprouted and grown to maturity, these seeds would form a new population of bean plants. 
            Seed weight is not only important in agriculture—it also plays a key role in wild plant ecology. 
            Heavier seeds often grow faster and survive better, but they require more resources from the parent plant. 
            This creates a tradeoff: plants that produce larger seeds typically produce fewer of them. 
            This balance between seed size and number is shaped by genetics, influenced by natural selection, and varies across species. 
            Measuring the <term>mean</term> and <term>variance</term> of seed weights helps us understand the reproductive strategy of the plant population.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Design a Sampling Strategy</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            Your sample will consist of <term>80 beans</term>. 
            Avoid simply grabbing the top layer of seeds from the bag, as this may result in a biased sample—larger or smaller seeds may have settled unevenly during transport. 
            Instead, spread the beans out on a clean lab bench, mix them thoroughly, and select the 80 beans closest to your side. 
            This approach helps ensure your sample is <term>random and representative</term>.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Weigh and Record Your Data</term> 
        </p>
        <p>
            Use a precision balance to weigh each of the 80 beans individually. 
            Record weights in <term>milligrams (mg)</term> for consistency and ease of analysis. 
            For example, a bean weighing 0.087 g should be recorded as 87 mg. 
            If your balance only measures to two decimal places, round accordingly (e.g., 0.09 g = 90 mg). 
            Enter all measurements into your laboratory worksheet.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Create a Hisotgram</term> 
        </p>
        <p>
            Using your recorded data, construct a <term>histogram</term> to visualize the distribution of seed weights. 
            Follow the format demonstrated in the lab introduction.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Calculate Descriptive Statistics</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            Compute the following statistics for your example:
        </p>
        <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>Mean</term>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>Standard Deviation (s)</term>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>Standard Error (S.E.)</term>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <term>95% Confidence Interval</term>
            </p>
        </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Interpret Your Results</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            Examine your histogram and statistics. 
            What do they reveal about variation in seed weight for this species? 
            Answer the <term>Discussion Questions</term> provided in your worksheet. 
            Consider whether natural selection would always produce a symmetrical distribution of seed weights.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Verify Your Sample Accuracy</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            With your lab group, count the total number of beans in the bag. 
            Then weigh the entire population using a plastic tray, subtracting the tray’s weight. 
            If the balance can't accommodate all beans at once, divide them into smaller groups, weigh each, and sum the totals. 
            Calculate the <term>population mean</term> by dividing the total weight by the number of beans. 
            Compare this population mean to your sample mean from Step 5. 
            Does the population mean fall within your calculated <term>95% confidence interval</term>?
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>

</section>